Programs this compiler answers differently from clang, kept as programs.

Every file here is a known wrong answer, so nothing in this directory gates
anything: `tests/open.sh` counts and always exits 0. That is the point of the
directory rather than a weakness of it. A defect put in tests/cases turns the
three boxes red until somebody mends it, and a defect written down only in
prose is one `tools/exclusions` cannot see and no run will ever mention - which
is how the tail of docs/audit-2026-09-06.html came to sit unworked underneath
four green suites for three days. This is the third place.

Each program carries a `<name>.clang` holding the oracle's answer: `REFUSE` on
a line of its own, or `ACCEPT` followed by the exact stdout, recorded with

    clang++ -x c++ -std=c++11 -pedantic-errors

A case is closed when cxx1 agrees with that file, and open.sh says so by name.
**A closed case does not belong here.** Move it to tests/cases with its
.expected or .error, where the suites will hold it from then on, and delete it
from this directory - a register that keeps its fixed entries stops being a
list of what is wrong.

Two rules about what may be added:

  - it must be a shape somebody writes. A defect nobody can reach is a note for
    docs/CONFORMANCE.md, not a program here.
  - where the defect is a leak, a double destroy, or a destructor on a slot
    nothing built, count objects with ../cases/lifetime.h and print the ledger.
    C++11 permits elision, so a constructor trace differs legitimately between
    two conforming compilers and only the balance says anything. See
    catch-return-end-catch.cpp, where cxx1 prints built=2 gone=1 live=1 and
    clang built=1 gone=1 live=0: the same program, one leaked exception object,
    and identical output above the ledger line.

None is here now (open.sh counts), and both halves of the movement below are the point. **Five of
the original fourteen have been mended and moved out** with their .expected,
which is the movement the rule above asks for: capture-default-this,
polymorphic-class-template-zts, catch-return-end-catch, and then
paren-direct-init-scalar and std-swap-two-ints together, one being the other's
cause. **Three arrived** while measuring those - mem-init-template-id-base,
lambda-return-through-try and catch-copy-throws. And **six over-acceptances
went in one round** - auto-two-declarators, const-member-default-init,
constructor-return-type, duplicate-data-member, member-pointer-const-object and
operator-plus-int-int - each becoming a refusal case in tests/cases with the
legal shape beside it in refusals-that-must-not-fire.cpp. two-d-array-member,
the only *miscompile* the register ever held, went with them. A register that only ever
grows is a list of complaints, and one that only ever shrinks is finished.
**virtual-base-dispatch arrived on 2026-09-15** from the third review: a
class with a virtual base had no function slots in its vtable, no secondary
vtable, VTT or virtual thunk, and no deleting destructor - the Itanium
machinery of virtual inheritance. **It left the same evening with
virtual-base-second-base and virtual-dtor-through-virtual-base**, the three
that were that machinery seen from three sides, when it was written
(CLAUDE.md, "Virtual inheritance proper"); all three sit in tests/cases.
**wchar-distinct left on 2026-09-15**, wchar_t being a Kind of its own now,
mangled `w`, an overload apart from int - the third review's T8, seen from
the link. **One arrived from the fourth target and left the same day** (2026-09-15):
dtor-throws-unwinding, a destructor that throws while another exception
unwinds, which the standard says terminates - the C6000 table had cl6x's
catch-and-terminate scope first, the Itanium hosts got a catch-all row whose
pad aborts a few hours later, and the case sits in tests/cases with only
x86_64-windows excused by name.

The original fourteen were re-measured on 2026-09-09 at 48f28a3, one program each,
from the tail of docs/audit-2026-09-06.html that the five roots did not cover -
the fourteen refusals of ordinary code, the twenty-seven over-acceptances and
the _ZTS assembler failure, none of which went with the roots. That report says
its own programs live in the reviewers' scratch directories; these are in the
repository, which is the difference between a finding and a test.

**The five that were left went on 2026-09-17**, each to tests/cases with its
.expected: mem-init-template-id-base (a template-id base is read as a type,
as the base-clause reads it), lambda-class-scope-names (closureScope_ records
the class a lambda was written in, `this` captured or not), catch-copy-throws
(a terminate scope over the catch's copy - and the program itself was
rewritten, since `throw E(1)` copied a temporary clang elides and cxx1 does
not, so the copy that threw was the throw's own and the outer handler was the
right answer; `throw g` from a static is nobody's to elide),
lambda-return-through-try (the whole body is read, every `return` reporting
its type) and constexpr-double (fold() hands a floating operand to foldDouble
where an integer comes out of it). **One arrived the same day and left the
same day**, found by the fourth of those: lambda-static-local, a static local
inside a lambda's body named `operator().n`, which no assembler takes - and
`~S.d` in a destructor, and one `f.n` for S::f, T::f, f(int) and f(double)
alike. A static local is named by its function's mangled symbol now, and the
case in tests/cases holds all of those shapes. An empty register is not a
finished one: the next program that answers differently from clang goes here.
